{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusader states of the Levant during the 12th and 13th centuries. He was a member of the Raymond family a powerful noble lineage originating from southern France. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and economic development within his county.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1155. Immediately he faced challenges including ongoing conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and rival Crusader lords. His early years focused on consolidating his authority and defending Tripoli's borders. He demonstrated military prowess and political acumen quickly establishing himself as a capable ruler. He strengthened Tripoli's defenses and fostered economic growth through trade agreements.\n\nRaymond III's rule was characterized by a complex relationship with other Crusader states particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He often aligned himself with the King of Jerusalem when it served his interests but also maintained a degree of independence. He participated in several major Crusades including the Third Crusade and the Fifth Crusade. His involvement in these campaigns expanded his reputation and influence throughout the Levant.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nRaymond III led numerous military campaigns against Muslim forces. He fought against the Ayyubids under Saladin and other local rulers. He engaged in sieges battles and skirmishes consistently defending Tripoli's territory. A significant event was his involvement in the defense of Jerusalem during Saladin's siege in 1187. Though Jerusalem fell Raymond III played a role in organizing the defense and negotiating with Saladin. He also conducted campaigns in the region of Antioch and the Levant securing strategic advantages for Tripoli. His military strategies often involved a combination of defensive fortifications and offensive raids.\n\n*Diplomacy and Administration*\n\nBeyond military matters Raymond III was a skilled diplomat. He negotiated treaties alliances and trade agreements with various powers including Muslim rulers and other Crusader states. He understood the importance of maintaining peaceful relations when possible to ensure Tripoli's prosperity. He fostered a vibrant cultural and economic environment within his county encouraging trade and attracting merchants. He invested in infrastructure including fortifications roads and ports enhancing Tripoli's stability and economic potential. His administration was known for its efficiency and fairness contributing to a period of relative stability and prosperity for the county. Raymond III's reign represents a crucial period in the history of Tripoli a testament to his leadership military skill and diplomatic abilities. He left a lasting legacy as one of the most important counts of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -1.674619436264038}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0688803493976593}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -1.1205610462639015e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.5748875486897305e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -5.602820692729438e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.15130580961704254}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.11288027465343475}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -7.390703103737906e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": "9", "logprob": -3.0397906812140718e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.027286473661661148}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.05794716253876686}, {